### schema.validateAsync(value, [options])

Source: https://github.com/hapijs/joi/blob/master/_autodocs/api-reference/any.md

Asynchronously validates a value. Returns the validated value or metadata, and rejects with a ValidationError on failure. Required if the schema contains external rules.

```js
validateAsync(value, options) {

    return Validator.entryAsync(value, this, options);
}
```

--------------------------------

### Joi schema with defaults, casting, and async validation

Source: https://github.com/hapijs/joi/blob/master/_autodocs/api-reference/any.md

Shows how to use Joi.object() with default values, type casting, and asynchronous validation handling.

```js
const Joi = require('joi');

const config = Joi.object({
    port: Joi.number().default(8080).cast('string'),
    env: Joi.string().valid('dev', 'prod')
}).with('port', 'env');

(async () => {
    try {
        const result = await config.validateAsync({});
        console.log(result.port, typeof result.port);   // 8080 'string'
    }
    catch (err) {
        console.error(err.message);
    }
})();
```

### object

Source: https://github.com/hapijs/joi/blob/master/API.md

Generates a schema object that matches an object data type. Defaults to allowing any child key.

Supports the same methods of the [`any()`](#any) type.

```js
const object = Joi.object({
    a: Joi.number().min(1).max(10).integer(),
    b: 'some string'
});

await object.validateAsync({ a: 5 });
```

Note that when an object schema type is passed as an input to another joi method (e.g. array
item) or is set as a key definition, the `Joi.object()` constructor may be omitted. For example:

```js
const schema = Joi.array().items({ a: Joi.string() });
```

Possible validation errors: [`object.base`](#objectbase)
